task.description:
Design a TEST-ONLY fix for GH-600 / ISS-0629: TC-LUA-10-03 in src/lua/limiter_wiring_test.zig (test at line 515, the final assertion at line 600: `try std.testing.expect(watchdog_state.hasFired());`) intermittently fails because it asserts on `watchdog_state.hasFired()` immediately after `lua_pcall` returns, with no allowance for the watchdog thread's own polling latency.

CONFIRMED ROOT CAUSE (verified against real source, not assumed): `WatchdogHandle.start()` (src/lua/timeout.zig L211-214) spawns `watchdogLoop` (L187-200) on its own `std.Thread`, completely separate from the Lua-owning thread running the test's `lua_pcall`. `watchdogLoop` polls in a `while(true)` loop: check `stop_requested`, compare wall-clock `now` against `deadline_ns`, and only if `now >= deadline` does it `state.fired.store(true, .release)` -- otherwise it calls `sleepNanos(POLL_INTERVAL_NS)` (L84: `POLL_INTERVAL_NS: u64 = 10 * std.time.ns_per_ms`, i.e. 10ms) and loops again. So after the 1s deadline passes, the watchdog thread can take up to ~10ms (its own poll interval) PLUS ordinary OS scheduling latency (the thread has to actually be scheduled back onto a core after its sleep) before `fired` flips true. The test's 200,000,000-iteration Lua loop reliably runs past the 1s deadline by design (comment at L567-574 confirms this is intentional), but the test then reads `watchdog_state.hasFired()` (L600) on the very next line after `lua_pcall` returns, with zero allowance for that ~10ms+ latency window. Under system load (CPU contention, e.g. from a concurrent `zig build test-lua` run or other processes), the watchdog thread's actual scheduling can be delayed well past its nominal 10ms poll interval, so the race window widens and the flake rate increases -- this matches the observed evidence exactly (ISS-0629: 3 fail / 2 pass across 5 consecutive runs, zero code changes). This is a genuine scheduling race in the TEST's assertion timing, NOT a logic defect in WatchdogState/WatchdogHandle/watchdogLoop -- the watchdog mechanism itself is correct per its own design (src/design/iss0169-lua08-09-10-limiter-wiring.md §2.5) and per the passing runs' own evidence (it does fire, just not always within the zero-tolerance window the test currently allows).

REQUIRED FIX SHAPE (test-only, src/lua/limiter_wiring_test.zig only -- do NOT touch src/lua/timeout.zig or any other production file):

Replace the single line at L600:
```
try std.testing.expect(watchdog_state.hasFired());
```
with a bounded retry/backoff loop that polls `hasFired()` periodically instead of asserting once:

```zig
// The watchdog thread flips `fired` asynchronously on its own poll cycle
// (src/lua/timeout.zig POLL_INTERVAL_NS = 10ms) -- give it a bounded window
// to observe the deadline that has already passed, rather than asserting
// immediately on this thread's own schedule. This absorbs scheduling
// jitter without masking a genuine watchdog failure: if `fired` is still
// false after 1000ms (100x the watchdog's own 10ms poll interval, and a
// full second past the already-elapsed 1s deadline), the watchdog is
// genuinely broken, not merely delayed, and the test must still fail.
var waited_ms: u64 = 0;
while (!watchdog_state.hasFired() and waited_ms < 1_000) {
    std.Thread.sleep(20 * std.time.ns_per_ms) catch {};
    waited_ms += 20;
}
try std.testing.expect(watchdog_state.hasFired());
```

NOTE ON `std.Thread.sleep`: timeout.zig's own doc comment (L96-104, CDV-0169-5) states `std.Thread.sleep` does NOT exist in this repo's Zig 0.16.0 stdlib and required a custom `sleepNanos` using platform primitives (`kernel32 Sleep` on Windows, `std.c.nanosleep` on POSIX). CODE-DESIGNER MUST verify the correct sleep primitive available to test code in this Zig version before finalizing the design -- either reuse/expose `timeout.zig`'s existing (currently private) `sleepNanos` helper (would require making it `pub` or adding a small test-seam wrapper), or use `std.Thread.yield()` in a spin-wait loop combined with a wall-clock deadline check via the same `milliTimestamp()` helper the test already imports and uses at L584/L586, e.g.:
```zig
const retry_deadline = milliTimestamp() + 1_000;
while (!watchdog_state.hasFired() and milliTimestamp() < retry_deadline) {
    std.Thread.yield() catch {};
}
try std.testing.expect(watchdog_state.hasFired());
```
This second form avoids the sleep-primitive question entirely by reusing `milliTimestamp()` (already imported/used in this exact test) and `std.Thread.yield()` (confirmed available on `std.Thread` per timeout.zig's own doc comment at L99). Prefer this form unless there is a reason to prefer discrete polling intervals over a yield-spin -- either is acceptable, but pick ONE and justify it in the design.

RETRY BOUND -- justified numbers (pick one approach and apply consistently):
- Poll/sleep interval: 20ms (2x the watchdog's own 10ms POLL_INTERVAL_NS -- comfortably coarser than the mechanism being observed, avoids busy-spinning, still fine-grained relative to the 1000ms budget) if using discrete sleep-based polling; OR plain `std.Thread.yield()` spin if using the yield-spin form (no separate interval to choose).
- Total timeout budget: 1000ms (1 full second). Justification: this is generous relative to the ~10ms nominal poll interval (100x headroom) so it comfortably absorbs realistic OS scheduling jitter under load (the kind of load that produced the observed 3/5 flake rate), while still being small relative to the test's own 30s upper bound on `elapsed_ms` (L591) -- so total test runtime impact is negligible even in the retry-exhausted failure case. Critically, it does NOT weaken the test's actual guarantee: the deadline already passed (by construction, the 200M-iteration loop takes far longer than 1s), so this retry loop only ever waits for the watchdog thread to catch up to a fact that is already true. A watchdog that never fires within a full additional second past its already-expired 1s deadline is not exhibiting scheduling jitter, it is broken, and the final `try std.testing.expect(watchdog_state.hasFired())` after the loop exits still correctly fails the test in that case.

SCOPE DECISION (explicit, made by ISSUE-FIXER, confirm or override with rationale if you disagree): GH-600 / ISS-0629 names TC-LUA-10-03 specifically, and TC-LUA-10-03 is the ONLY test in this file (or found anywhere else in src/) with this race pattern. TC-LUA-10-01 (L219) does not call `hasFired()` at all -- it asserts on the in-VM hook's timeout error path, unrelated to the watchdog thread. TC-LUA-10-02 (L347) does exercise the watchdog via `platformCallService` -> `host_context.zig activeWatchdogFired()` (L173-177) -> `wd.hasFired()` (L176), but that call happens SYNCHRONOUSLY inline within the same blocking host-call chain that the test's own assertions key off of (`result.success`/`error_message`, L430-432), not as an independent post-hoc read of an atomic set by an unrelated poll thread's own schedule -- its lower-bound assertion (`elapsed_ms >= 1_400`, L426) already proves the check ran after the mock's 1500ms delay elapsed, well past the 1s deadline, with no analogous race window. The `never_started.hasFired()` check at L632 (TC-LUA-10-03's own mutation-check test) asserts FALSE with no watchdog thread ever spawned at all -- nothing can flip that flag, so there is no race to fix there either. CONCLUSION: this fix is correctly scoped to TC-LUA-10-03 alone (L600) -- no other test needs the same treatment. Do not expand scope beyond this single assertion.
